INT. WINE CELLAR BUNKER - FRENCH CHATEAU - NIGHT

Barrel vaults. Cobwebbed racks of dusty bottles. A single carbide lamp. The ground trembles faintly; every so often a spill of dust sifts from the stones overhead. Shells are falling somewhere above.

SGT. BARAKA (40s, broad, helmet on his knee, cigarette cupped in his fist) sits on a barrel. LT. HOLLOWAY (20s, pistol, pressed tunic, a cut over one eye) studies a hand-drawn map. CPL. FINNEGAN (30s, freckled, rifle across his thighs) cleans his boots. PVT. ORLANDO (teen, helmet too big) shares a canteen with PVT. DAGNY (20s, red-cross armband, bandage roll in her teeth).

Nobody talks. Another tremor. Dust sifts. A bottle clinks in the rack. Dagny glances up, lips tight around the bandage.

SGT. BARAKA
They're walking it down the hill.

LT. HOLLOWAY
Sergeant, we'll hold until dawn.

SGT. BARAKA
Sir, we'll hold until the roof does.

CPL. FINNEGAN
How many bottles you reckon are down here?

PVT. ORLANDO
Hundreds.

CPL. FINNEGAN
Could drink the war away.

PVT. DAGNY
Could drown in it, more like.

LT. HOLLOWAY
I want the radio checked.

SGT. BARAKA ^
I've checked it twice.

CPL. FINNEGAN
The radio's fine, sir.

The field radio on the barrel beside Holloway hisses.

MAJOR TREVELYAN (V.O.)
(distorted)
Holloway. Report.

LT. HOLLOWAY
(into the handset)
Wine Cellar here, sir. All present. All quiet.

MAJOR TREVELYAN (V.O.)
That is correct. The shelling will cease at oh-four-hundred. Stay under.

LT. HOLLOWAY
Understood, sir.

He sets the handset down. Nobody breathes.

PVT. ORLANDO
Why would they stop?

PVT. DAGNY
They don't stop.

SGT. BARAKA
That's the point.

Silence. Orlando tilts his head. Frowns. The tremors have stopped. The dust no longer sifts. Nothing in the whole vault moves.

CPL. FINNEGAN
Four o'clock?

LT. HOLLOWAY
Not yet.

PVT. DAGNY
Then why is it quiet?

Baraka crushes his cigarette. Holloway folds the map. From above, faint but clear, comes the clank of a tank tread on gravel, slow, deliberate, unhurried.

SGT. BARAKA
Because they're finished shelling.
